'''希望と生存・幸福の会''' (''Kibō to Seizon・Kōfuku no Kai'', "Hope and Survival Happiness Group") was a women-only cult and survivalist commune founded in [[Toyozawa]], [[Okaiken]], in 1993. The group became active in [[Balboa]] in the mid-1990s after purchasing land in [[Warakú comarca]] of [[Estret Province]], where it established a fortified agricultural compound. It became notorious after Balboan authorities discovered livestock theft, crop theft, coercive labour practices, and an armed standoff with the Balboan military, which had been triggered by the discovery of firearms linked to criminal networks in [[Tramuntana]]. The [[History of Okaiken#The_Lost_Decade (1993–2000)|Lost Decade]] (1993–2000), a period of economic stagnation, corporate retrenchment, and social dislocation that produced a wave of self-help circles, purity movements, and alternative religious communities. In its early years, the Hope and Survival Happiness Group presented itself as a women-only refuge from unemployment, domestic insecurity, and the erosion of lifetime employment. By the time it was dismantled in Balboa, however, it had become a closed and increasingly coercive enclave.

== History ==

=== Origins in Okaiken ===

The group was founded by [[Mizue Naruse]], a former stockbroker from Toyozawa who began holding informal study circles for women in 1993, shortly after the beginning of Okaiken's Lost Decade. Naruse's early teachings combined survivalist rhetoric, communal labour, and a heavily moralised form of self-help. Members were encouraged to abandon what Naruse called the "false comforts" of the corporate household and to build a new life around discipline, shared work, and emotional severance from men, employers, and extended families.

The 1990s in Okaiken were marked by the collapse of the postwar employment model. After years of asset-price inflation and then a sudden economic reversal, companies cut long-held guarantees, young workers found themselves in unstable part-time jobs, and rural communities lost both population and investment. In this atmosphere, Naruse's message found an audience among women who had been laid off from service work, widowed by industrial accidents, or alienated from family expectations. Her movement grew first as a network of domestic meeting groups and later as a full-time commune.

Unlike larger Okaiken sects that emphasised revelation or prophecy, the Hope and Survival Happiness Group framed itself as an answer to material insecurity. Its members taught that "hope" meant the discipline to survive, "survival" meant collective living, and "happiness" meant obedience to the group. That slogan became the basis of the cult's public identity, and it was repeated in handwritten pamphlets, recorded sermons, and the banners hung in its compounds.

=== Expansion into Balboa ===

By 1995, Naruse's movement had outgrown its rented halls in Toyozawa and was looking for land outside Okaiken. According to later testimony, the leadership viewed [[Balboa]] as ideal: land in the interior was cheaper than in the coastal cities, oversight was weak in the Warakú comarca, and the region's patchwork of ranches and small farms offered both concealment and opportunities for self-sufficiency.

The group purchased a tract of land in [[Estret]] and established a compound of dormitories, greenhouses, storage sheds, and a central meeting hall that members referred to as the House of Hope. The commune was formally women-only, though men occasionally appeared as contractors, drivers, or messengers. New arrivals were required to surrender personal property, cut contact with family members, and adopt communal schedules built around fieldwork, prayer, and instruction sessions delivered by Naruse and her inner circle.

The compound initially presented itself as a rural development project. Naruse advertised the group as a place where women could learn food production, textile work, and mutual defence against what she called the "collapse of the old order." Local officials in Warakú initially regarded the settlement as a harmless foreign commune, especially because the group maintained orderly gardens and paid its land taxes.

Over time, however, the compound became increasingly insular. Former members later described sleep deprivation, punitive fasting, and a rigid internal hierarchy in which senior women controlled access to food, medicine, and contact with the outside world. The group also developed a habit of collecting goods from neighbouring farms under what it described as "survival requisition." Livestock disappeared from nearby corrals, crops were taken from irrigation plots, and tools were borrowed and not returned. These incidents were initially treated as rural thefts, but they later formed part of the criminal case against the cult.

=== Criminal allegations ===

By the late 1990s, farmers in Estret and neighbouring settlements were reporting repeated losses of goats, chickens, maize, and stored grain. Some claimed that members of the commune would arrive at night in small groups, while others said the cult's field teams openly intercepted produce trucks and redirected food supplies to the compound.

Investigators later alleged that the group financed part of its expansion through black-market contacts and through intermediaries connected to drug cartels in [[Tramuntana]]. Those links were reportedly used to obtain cash, fuel, and firearms, although the group denied acting as a cartel proxy. The weapons matter became central to the eventual raid: when Balboan police learned that the commune possessed rifles and ammunition, the case escalated from a rural property dispute into a national security issue.

The cult's leadership insisted that the weapons were for self-defence. Naruse framed the Balboan state as an instrument of decay and told followers that the commune must be prepared for "the last hunger," a phrase that became common in the compound's internal sermons. To critics, the rhetoric looked less like survivalism than a justification for armed isolation.

=== The Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff ===

The Balboan military raid on the Estret compound, commonly known as the [[Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff]], was launched after local police and agricultural inspectors compiled evidence of theft, unlawful detention, and illegal weapons possession. The confrontation left three people dead. When troops arrived, the cult refused immediate entry and barricaded the compound, turning the operation into a tense standoff.

The confrontation became one of the most discussed rural security incidents in 1990s Balboa. Residents of nearby farms were evacuated, and roads in the Warakú comarca were closed while negotiators attempted to secure a surrender. The military later said the cult had access to firearms sufficient to threaten a prolonged siege, though the weapons were largely outdated and poorly maintained.

After a period of negotiation, the group surrendered. Several members were injured during the raid and subsequent arrests. Naruse and senior lieutenants were taken into custody, and the compound was later dismantled. Balboan prosecutors described the case as a mixture of organised theft, illegal arms possession, coercive confinement, and religious abuse.

=== Aftermath ===

The [[Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff]] had a lasting impact on Balboan public policy. It led to greater scrutiny of foreign religious communes, tighter oversight of land purchases by outside groups, and a more aggressive stance toward rural militias in the interior. In Warakú, the compound became a warning story told by farmers and local officials whenever new groups sought isolated land for communal projects.

In Okaiken, the cult was later cited as a product of the Lost Decade: a period in which economic pessimism, weakened social institutions, and the search for personal meaning created a fertile environment for survivalist organisations. Historians of religion often place the group alongside other 1990s Okaiken sects that converted social anxiety into rigid communal discipline, though few matched the Hope and Survival Happiness Group's combination of female exclusivity, agrarian isolation, and criminal escalation.

Former members were divided in their later accounts. Some described the commune as a place of refuge before it became a prison; others said the movement had been coercive from the beginning and simply became more openly violent once it left Okaiken. That ambiguity has remained part of the group's legacy.

== Beliefs and organisation ==

The group's internal teaching combined three themes: survival, obedience, and purification. Naruse argued that modern society was collapsing under the weight of consumerism, male violence, and corporate exhaustion, and that women could only endure by building small disciplined communities outside the family and the market. Members were expected to farm, mend clothes, prepare food collectively, and attend nightly study sessions.

The movement's women-only structure was central to its appeal. Many recruits were drawn by the promise of a space free from domestic abuse, workplace insecurity, and patriarchal control. Once inside, however, members encountered a rigid hierarchy in which Naruse's inner circle controlled property, movement, and communication. The group rejected marriage, discouraged childbirth, and treated outside contact as a threat to communal purity.

Although the cult's public language was rooted in care and mutual aid, its private life revolved around obedience and scarcity. Food rations, sleep schedules, and work assignments were used as disciplinary tools. Former members described the commune as both a refuge from the Lost Decade and a miniature version of the harsh social order it claimed to reject.

== Legacy ==

The Hope and Survival Happiness Group is remembered as one of the more notorious Okaikenese movements to spread into Balboa during the 1990s. In Okaiken, it is associated with the Lost Decade's spiritual experimentation and with the era's proliferation of women-led self-help communities that could, under pressure, become authoritarian.

In Balboa, the Estret incident became shorthand for the dangers of isolated foreign communes, weak rural oversight, and the intersection of local poverty with transnational criminal networks. The case is still cited in debates over land purchases in remote comarcas, and the ruined compound outside Estret remains a symbol of the period's social anxieties.

